  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Valley rain and mountain snow Sunday into Monday morning,\n   focused along and south of I-90. \n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions and valley fog next\n   week. \n\nGOES Satellite imagery this afternoon highlights a closed low\nspinning off the Oregon and Washington coastline, with southerly\nflow aloft sending mid-high level clouds over the Northern\nRockies. Regional radar imagery shows shower activity across \nsouth-central Idaho, with high-resolution guidance suggesting this\nactivity will shift northward through Lemhi County and west-\ncentral Montana into this evening. Overall, this shower activity \nwill bring hit or miss rain amounts of a trace to up to 0.10 \ninches.\n\nOn Saturday, the aforementioned closed low begins to move\nsoutheastward along the California coast. Shower activity will\nshift south of the area, with the majority of locations seeing\nmostly cloudy skies and dry conditions for Halloween activities.\nThe exception will be across southern portions of Idaho and Lemhi\nCounties and along the divide in southwest Montana, where\nscattered showers will bring light precipitation amounts. \n\nThe closed low will move across the Great Basin on Sunday, with\nmoisture and lift focusing precipitation along and south of the \nI-90 corridor. The majority of precipitation will fall with snow \nlevels ranging from 6,000-7,000 feet during the day on Sunday. As \nthe low begins to pivot eastward into the central Rockies Sunday \nnight, cold air advection aloft under northwesterly flow will \nbring lower snow levels, with snow falling down near 5,000 feet as\nprecipitation rates begin to decrease through Monday morning. \nOverall, this doesn't appear to be a significant snow event, with \nminor snow amounts (trace to locally up to 4 inches) above 6,000 \nfeet across southwest Montana and Lemhi County. This includes \nthe US-93 and I-90 corridor over Lost Trail and Homestake Passes. \n\nEnsemble model guidance is in strong agreement for high pressure\nto build across the western US Monday night into midweek. This\nstable pattern will lead to morning fog and stratus across many\nvalley areas. The weather pattern through much of the week will \nbe characterized by a split flow regime, with weaker systems \nattempting to break through the ridge of high pressure by \nThursday into Friday, perhaps bringing periodic showers to the \narea. No significant and/or strong systems are currently on the \nhorizon through next weekend.",
